# SECURITY — Difficulty 3-5 (advanced scenarios)
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
questions += [
  {"question_text":"A developer's application uses API Gateway with a Lambda authorizer. They need to pass the authenticated user's ID to the backend Lambda without the backend re-validating the token. How?","options":["The Lambda authorizer returns a policy with context variables that API Gateway passes to the backend in the request context","The backend Lambda must always re-validate the token independently for security","Store the user ID in a shared DynamoDB table that both the authorizer and backend read from","Pass the user ID as a query parameter that the authorizer appends to the request URL"],"correct_answer":"The Lambda authorizer returns a policy with context variables that API Gateway passes to the backend in the request context","explanation":"Lambda authorizers can return context key-value pairs alongside the IAM policy. API Gateway passes these in event.requestContext.authorizer. The backend trusts these values since they come from the authorizer, not the client.","memory_technique":"Authorizer context = Trusted metadata passed to backend. Authorizer sets userId, role, etc. Backend reads from requestContext.authorizer. No re-validation needed.","topic_area":"Security","difficulty_level":4,"it_context_mapping":"Authorizer context is like request attributes set by authentication middleware — trusted metadata available to downstream handlers."},
  {"question_text":"A developer needs to implement token refresh for a mobile app using Cognito. The access token expires in 1 hour. How should the app handle expiration without forcing re-login?","options":["Store the user's password and automatically re-authenticate when the token expires","Use the refresh token to obtain new access and ID tokens without user interaction","Request access tokens with a longer expiration time (24 hours) to reduce refresh frequency","Implement a background timer that refreshes the token 5 minutes before expiration using the ID token"],"correct_answer":"Use the refresh token to obtain new access and ID tokens without user interaction","explanation":"Cognito issues a refresh token (valid for 30 days by default) alongside access/ID tokens. When the access token expires, the app sends the refresh token to Cognito's token endpoint to get new tokens silently — no re-login needed.","memory_technique":"Refresh token = Silent re-auth. Access token expires (1hr) → send refresh token → get new access token. No password needed. Refresh token valid 30 days.","topic_area":"Security","difficulty_level":3,"it_context_mapping":"Refresh tokens are the standard OAuth2 pattern — long-lived tokens that obtain new short-lived access tokens without user interaction."},
  {"question_text":"A developer needs to restrict a Lambda function so it can only be invoked from a specific VPC (not from the internet or other VPCs). How should they implement this?","options":["Place the Lambda function inside the VPC — this automatically restricts invocation to that VPC only","Use a Lambda resource-based policy with a condition on aws:SourceVpc to restrict invocations to the specific VPC","Configure the Lambda function's security group to only allow inbound traffic from the VPC CIDR","Use IAM policy conditions on the caller's role to restrict where they can invoke Lambda from"],"correct_answer":"Use a Lambda resource-based policy with a condition on aws:SourceVpc to restrict invocations to the specific VPC","explanation":"Lambda resource policies support VPC conditions (aws:SourceVpc, aws:SourceVpce). This restricts who can invoke the function based on the network origin — only requests from the specified VPC or VPC endpoint are allowed.","memory_technique":"aws:SourceVpc condition on Lambda resource policy = Only callable from that VPC. Network-level invocation restriction. Not the same as Lambda being IN a VPC.","topic_area":"Security","difficulty_level":5,"it_context_mapping":"VPC-based invocation restrictions are like network segmentation — ensuring services can only be called from trusted network locations."},
  {"question_text":"A developer is implementing a zero-trust architecture where every service call must be authenticated and authorised, even within the same AWS account. What combination of AWS features enables this?","options":["VPC security groups that restrict traffic between services at the network level","IAM roles with least-privilege policies + resource-based policies on every service + VPC endpoints for private communication","AWS PrivateLink between all services to ensure encrypted communication","AWS Service Mesh with App Mesh for mTLS between all service components"],"correct_answer":"IAM roles with least-privilege policies + resource-based policies on every service + VPC endpoints for private communication","explanation":"Zero-trust on AWS: (1) Each service has its own IAM role with minimal permissions. (2) Resource-based policies on Lambda/SQS/S3 restrict which roles can access them. (3) VPC endpoints keep traffic private. Every call is authenticated via SigV4.","memory_technique":"Zero-trust on AWS = IAM everywhere. Each service has unique role. Resource policies restrict callers. VPC endpoints for private paths. SigV4 authenticates every call.","topic_area":"Security","difficulty_level":5,"it_context_mapping":"Zero-trust with IAM is like mTLS service mesh — every service-to-service call is authenticated and authorised, regardless of network location."},
  {"question_text":"A developer's Lambda function needs to access resources in two different AWS accounts (read S3 in Account A, write DynamoDB in Account B). How should they configure permissions?","options":["Create a single IAM role that has cross-account permissions to both accounts","Have the Lambda assume different roles for each account using STS AssumeRole — one role per account","Use resource-based policies on both the S3 bucket and DynamoDB table to allow the Lambda's role","Store access keys for both accounts in Secrets Manager and use them directly"],"correct_answer":"Have the Lambda assume different roles for each account using STS AssumeRole — one role per account","explanation":"The Lambda function assumes a role in Account A for S3 access, then assumes a different role in Account B for DynamoDB access. Each role has minimal permissions for its specific task. The Lambda's execution role needs sts:AssumeRole permission for both target roles.","memory_technique":"Multi-account access = AssumeRole per account. Lambda role → AssumeRole(Account A role) for S3 → AssumeRole(Account B role) for DynamoDB. Separate credentials per account.","topic_area":"Security","difficulty_level":4,"it_context_mapping":"Multi-account role assumption is like federated identity across domains — obtaining separate credentials for each trust boundary."},
  {"question_text":"A developer needs to ensure that all data written to an SQS queue is encrypted and that only specific Lambda functions can decrypt and read the messages. What should they configure?","options":["Enable SQS SSE with a customer-managed KMS key and grant decrypt permission only to the consumer Lambda's execution role","Use client-side encryption in the producer and share the encryption key via Secrets Manager with consumers","Enable SQS SSE with the AWS-managed SQS key (aws/sqs) for automatic encryption","Configure the SQS queue policy to only allow specific Lambda functions to receive messages"],"correct_answer":"Enable SQS SSE with a customer-managed KMS key and grant decrypt permission only to the consumer Lambda's execution role","explanation":"SQS SSE with a customer-managed KMS key encrypts messages at rest. The KMS key policy grants kms:Decrypt only to specific Lambda execution roles. Other roles can't read messages even if they have SQS:ReceiveMessage permission — they can't decrypt.","memory_technique":"SQS SSE + customer KMS key = Encryption + access control. KMS key policy controls who can decrypt. Even with SQS access, no decrypt = can't read messages. Double lock.","topic_area":"Security","difficulty_level":4,"it_context_mapping":"KMS-based access control is like encryption-based authorization — even with network access, you need the decryption key to read the data."},
  {"question_text":"A developer discovers that their application's Cognito User Pool has been targeted by a credential stuffing attack (thousands of failed login attempts from different IPs). What Cognito feature helps mitigate this?","options":["Enable Cognito Advanced Security Features with adaptive authentication and compromised credentials detection","Implement a custom Lambda trigger that blocks IPs after 5 failed attempts","Enable MFA for all users which prevents credential stuffing entirely","Configure API Gateway WAF rules to block requests from known bot IP ranges"],"correct_answer":"Enable Cognito Advanced Security Features with adaptive authentication and compromised credentials detection","explanation":"Cognito Advanced Security uses ML to detect unusual sign-in attempts (new device, new location, impossible travel). It can require additional verification (MFA challenge) for risky attempts and block compromised credentials found in public breaches.","memory_technique":"Cognito Advanced Security = ML-based threat detection. Risk scoring per login attempt. Adaptive MFA challenges. Compromised credential checking. Blocks credential stuffing.","topic_area":"Security","difficulty_level":4,"it_context_mapping":"Cognito Advanced Security is like adaptive authentication in enterprise IAM — using risk signals to step up authentication requirements."},
]

